2) Fix the ARM/Bicep template.
- Consumption (Microsoft.Logic/workflows):
  - System-assigned:
    "identity": { "type": "SystemAssigned" }
  - User-assigned:
    variables: { "uaIdentityId": "/subscriptions/<sub>/resourceGroups/<rg>/providers/Microsoft.ManagedIdentity/userAssignedIdentities/<name>" }
    "identity": {
      "type": "UserAssigned",
      "userAssignedIdentities": {
        "[variables('uaIdentityId')]": {}
      }
    }
- Standard (Microsoft.Web/sites for your Logic App):
  - System-assigned:
    "identity": { "type": "SystemAssigned" }
  - User-assigned:
    variables: { "uaIdentityId": "/subscriptions/<sub>/resourceGroups/<rg>/providers/Microsoft.ManagedIdentity/userAssignedIdentities/<name>" }
    "identity": {
      "type": "UserAssigned",
      "userAssignedIdentities": {
        "[variables('uaIdentityId')]": {}
      }
    }
3) Redeploy the template and restart the app (Standard):
- For Standard: restart the site after identity changes to ensure the host picks up the new identity.
4) Grant RBAC to the identity on target resources and reauthorize connections as needed:
- Get principalId from the resource’s identity.principalId (or list the user-assigned identity’s principalId).
- Assign roles at the correct scope (e.g., Key Vault Secrets User, Storage Blob Data Reader, etc.).
- For existing API connections created with a previous identity, open each connection and Reauthorize using Managed Identity (or recreate the connection) so it binds to the current identity.
5) Align workflow/connection auth settings:
- Ensure actions using managed identity specify the correct auth (e.g., "authentication": { "type": "ManagedServiceIdentity" }).
- If you intentionally set identity.type to "None", switch those actions/connections to use alternative auth (service principal/OAuth/keys) and remove MSI auth blocks.
6) Validate and monitor:
